{% call m.feat({ eyebrow: 'Five categories', title: 'Numbers, ordinals, fractions,<br>dates, times — Once · Auto',
  shots: [ { src: shots + '01_main_menu.webp', alt: 'Home — five categories with Once and Auto buttons', w: 720, h: 1560 } ] }) %}
  <p>The digits are the same everywhere — but saying them out loud follows different rules, and that's where you stall. Pick one of five categories from the home screen, then listen one of two ways.</p>
  <ul class="checks">
    <li><strong>Once</strong> — type any value and hear it read once</li>
    <li><strong>Auto</strong> — let the number count up on its own and listen on repeat</li>
    <li>The top of the screen shows whether <strong>voice data</strong> is installed</li>
  </ul>
{% endcall %}

{% call m.feat({ eyebrow: 'Auto', title: 'Not a quiz:<br>relaxed, repeated listening', soft: true, flip: true,
  shots: [ { src: shots + '02_number_auto.webp', alt: 'Numbers · Auto — 48,500 in Korean and English with step and repeat options', w: 720, h: 1560 } ] }) %}
  <p>English groups digits by three (thousand · million · billion); Korean groups by four (만 man · 억 eok · 조 jo). Every value appears in both on one line — 48,500 shows as "사만 팔천오백 · forty-eight thousand five hundred." Set a start, press play, and the number climbs while the voice keeps up.</p>
  <ul class="checks">
    <li><strong>Step</strong> — +1, +10, +100, +thousand … up to billions</li>
    <li><strong>Repeat</strong> — 5, 15, 65 or 115 times</li>
    <li>English always on; turn on Korean to hear <strong>Korean then English</strong></li>
  </ul>
{% endcall %}

{% call m.feat({ eyebrow: 'Ordinals · Fractions', title: '11th, 12th, 13th,<br>and three fifths',
  shots: [
    { src: shots + '03_ordinal.webp', alt: 'Ordinals · Auto — 45,200th', w: 720, h: 1560 },
    { src: shots + '04_fraction.webp', alt: 'Fraction · Auto — natural number, numerator, denominator', w: 720, h: 1560 }
  ] }) %}
  <p>Ordinals are full of exceptions, and fractions follow the cardinal-over-ordinal rule. The app reads both correctly.</p>
  <ul class="checks">
    <li><strong>-st / -nd / -rd / -th</strong>, first · second · third, 11th · 12th · 13th, large ordinals like 45,200th</li>
    <li>Three parts — natural number · numerator · denominator — "3/5" as <strong>"three fifths"</strong></li>
    <li>Mixed numbers add and — "8 and 3/5"; the Korean reading ("5분의 3") alongside</li>
  </ul>
{% endcall %}

{% call m.feat({ eyebrow: 'Dates · Time', title: 'the first of April<br>twenty twenty-six', soft: true, flip: true,
  shots: [
    { src: shots + '05_date.webp', alt: 'Date · Auto — pick from the calendar', w: 720, h: 1560 },
    { src: shots + '06_time.webp', alt: 'Time · Auto — pick from the clock', w: 720, h: 1560 }
  ] }) %}
  <p>Dates are read as "the &lt;ordinal&gt; of &lt;month&gt; &lt;year&gt;"; on the hour, time reads "o'clock," otherwise hour then minutes. The year is the hard part, and it's read the natural way.</p>
  <ul class="checks">
    <li>2026 → <strong>"twenty twenty-six"</strong>, 2010 → "twenty ten", 2000 → "two thousand"</li>
    <li>Type it or pick from the <strong>calendar · clock</strong>; the Korean reading is shown next to it</li>
    <li>On Auto, step by <strong>+minute / +hour</strong> to hear time expressions on repeat</li>
  </ul>
{% endcall %}

{% call m.feat({ eyebrow: 'Settings', title: 'Fine-tune the voice<br>until it sits right',
  shots: [ { src: shots + '07_settings.webp', alt: 'Settings — voice pitch, speed, listening interval', w: 720, h: 1560 } ] }) %}
  <p>Adjust the reading until it sits comfortably in your ear.</p>
  <ul class="checks">
    <li><strong>Voice pitch</strong> — a little lower / normal / a little higher</li>
    <li><strong>Voice speed</strong> — five steps, from slow to very fast</li>
    <li><strong>Listening interval</strong> — long / normal / short pause before the next number</li>
  </ul>
{% endcall %}

{% call m.more('See all features', 'Once mode · Why numbers trip you up') %}
  <div>
    <h4>Once mode</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Type a value and hear it once, in Korean or English</li>
      <li>Break a big number into digit groups to hear exactly where it splits at million and thousand</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div>
    <h4>Why numbers trip you up</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Different grouping — every three digits in English, every four in Korean ("48,500" is "사만 팔천오백")</li>
      <li>Irregular ordinals — first, second, third, plus 11th/12th/13th</li>
      <li>Set phrases — "the first of April", "o'clock", "three fifths" look different from the digits</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
{% endcall %}

